 limited funding to pay for  another cell culture incubator  the cell locker system is a novel  innovation which is designed into our  newest line of co2 incubators the  thermal scientific carousel BIOS and  thermo scientific for mastery cycle 160  litre incubators as shown here the  traditional incubator is subdivided into  six polycarbonate chambers which can  stay in the incubator or they can slide  out each cell actor has its own door but  the full inner incubator door can also  be open for cleaning the incubator  itself features a proven 12 log dry heat  sterilization cycle in chamber hepa  filtration and sensors and covered  protected water reservoir the entire  incubator chamber can also be optional  100 percent pure copper  each cell locker itself is made of the  same polycarbonate that makes up most  cell culture dishes and it contains an  optional transport door in case you want  to carry the cell Locker chamber out of  the incubator protecting yourselves from  the incubator all the way across to the  hood or down the hall to another lab the  cell lockers are autoclavable up to a  maximum of 12 times  both sides of each cell locker chamber  include an easily replaceable 0.2  micrometer membrane filter which permits  air circulation but prevents  transmission of particles into or out of  the cell lockers when the door is closed  so each cell locker is essentially a  small quarantine incubator for isolating  projects users or cell types  the cell locker system is easily  configured to fit the way your lab works  you can use it to segregate different  cell types users or experiments to  isolate and protect them from other work  for example if you previously had one  incubator that was only for induced  pluripotent stem cells now you can keep  these cells in the same incubator with  your other cell types with no worries  saving space in the lab and reducing the  number of incubator stacks you need to  gain access to yourself simply open the  outer heated incubator door and then  open the smaller individual inner door  for each cell locker  to give you an idea of the size each  cell locker chamber holds 9t 75 flasks  or up to 28 multi-well plates an  optional sliding work tray gives you  easy access to your flasks dishes or  plates  each chamber includes the optional  sliding work tray and that includes some  tip protection to protect your dishes  each cell Locker also includes the  optional transport door if you never  remove the cell lock or chamber from the  incubator you probably would not use the  transport door but if you want to carry  an individual cell lock or to your cell  culture hood or to a lab down the hall  the transport door would extend the  protection of the cell Locker  as you carry your cell culture vessels  so now you have a better understanding  of the cell locker system let me show  you why we invented the cell locker  system and some data from our extensive  testing to show how it will protect your  stem cells from contamination and  preserve their ideal culturing  environment  we know from our own surveys of more  than a thousand people globally that  about 87% of scientists culturing cells  experienced at least one contamination  event each year and many of us have more  than one event each year there are some  reasons for this but a big one is that  contamination comes from us humans carry  an average of 10,000 microorganisms per  square centimeter on our skin and from  those normal flora combined with  microorganisms in the environment even  in nor air carries between 30 and 1000  microorganisms per cubic meter  those are the most common cell culture  contaminant with up to 15% of cultures  in the United States contaminated up to  80% contamination in Japan and an  average of 35% contamination in Europe  and the rest of the world  we know that Michael plasma is caused  widespread effects because of their  ubiquity and their effects on cells they  are the most serious contaminant for  cultured cells  Mikell plasmas come from humans and from  other cultures technically michael  plasmas are bacteria but they are much  smaller than most bacteria and so they  can grow in your cultures to very high  densities without detection  also michael plasmas have no cell wall  so they are resistant to most  antibiotics which are directed against  the peptidoglycan in the bacterial cell  wall  Mikell plasmas affect the host cells  metabolism and may affect the morphology  they can cause chromosomal alterations  and damage the DNA and provokes  cytopathic responses in the cells all of  these alterations mean that your cells  will not respond normally in your  downstream assays the only way to know  for sure if your cells are carrying  mycoplasma is to test regularly  statistics show that the incidence of  mycoplasma is inversely proportional to  the amount of testing because if you  test you can detect a contamination and  limit the spread and the damage  so we thought about how much a  mycoplasma contamination could cost the  stem-cell lab of course this cost  estimate varies widely depending on the  lab itself how many people are involved  the time required to fight a  contamination the type and cost of the  stem cells and so on in short all these  costs are higher when working with stem  cells than with immortalised cell lines  and if the contamination spreads across  the lab those costs will rapidly  escalate for example one lab said they  spent as much as 100,000 u.s. dollars  fighting a mycoplasma outbreak  here I'm showing a more typical example  of costs to one stem-cell researcher who  spent one month dealing with a  mycoplasma contamination of her human  stem cells a new vial of cells cost  $1,000 u.s. and a mycoplasma  test kit cost $300 she figures she spent  more than two thousand dollars on  materials in reagents over that month  and her salary for one month is four  thousand US dollars and all her other  works stopped because all she could do  was work to eliminate this contamination  so you can see why we invented the cell  Locker to protect cells from  contamination  the cell locker chamber  prevents entry or exit of microorganisms  we wanted to ensure that we had a lot of  proof of this claim that microorganisms  could not pass through the filters which  protect the cell locker chambers so we  sent a cell locker system to two  independent test labs one at health  protection England important down in the  UK and one at IBF II in Germany all told  these independent labs performed over 15  rounds of testing with three very small  bacteria including Staphylococcus aureus  which is about 0.8 micrometers in size  breven demonise de minuto which is about  0.2 micrometers and is the accepted test  organism for testing 0.2 micrometer pore  size and finally mycoplasma  RL one of the most common cell culture  contaminants which ranges from 0.1 to  0.6 microns in size these companies and  labs circulated thousands of  microorganisms into the air circulation  of the incubator containing the cell  locker system for up to an hour at a  time  in all these tests not one bacterial  colony no growth at all was able to  enter or exit a closed cell locker but  hundreds of microorganisms were  collected inside the incubator but  outside the cell lockers in one other  type of test which I'm not showing here  these researchers took four individual  cell locker chambers out of the  incubator system and put them in a  bombardment cabinet one of these cell  lockers had a leaky door gasket and  another had the door propped open about  one centimeter in both of these  compromised cell lockers microorganisms  could get in through the air space  around the transport door but in the  properly closed cell lockers zero growth  was found inside together these tests  prove that the cell locker system and  the membrane filters protect cells  incubated inside from cross  contamination  so I know that we've all heard that when  filtering liquids with a 0