o conditions and changes in  temperature atmosphere humidity  nutrients and stimulants or repressors  in the medium and of course proximity of  other cells and cell responses will  change depending on these cues so  variations in any of these can cause  varied cell responses in differential  gene or protein expression or whether  the cells enter the growth site growth  cycle whether they grow faster or slower  or they stay quiet so this is another  consideration we thought about in  designing the cell locker system we  wanted the cells to be in the most ideal  environment possible for the maximum  time possible  let me share with you what the co2  incubator provides and growing yourself  and how variation would affect the cells  first of course we think of temperature  the incubator is generally set to 37  degrees Celsius to mimic human or animal  body temperature we know that most cells  are generally okay for a few days at 4  degrees but higher temperatures are  detrimental to cells most cells cannot  withstand more than a few hours of  temperatures 2 degrees Celsius above  normal and for many cells even one  degree higher results in cell death at  the same time lower temperatures result  in slower cell growth cells may even  enter growth stasis  next carbon dioxide which many of us  know is provided to balance the growth  medium to a neutral pH but did you know  that the amount of co2 we use in the  incubator which is most often set at 5%  is dependent upon the amount of buffer  provided by the growth medium and 5% co2  equates to 40 millimeters of mercury  which is the amount of co2 pressure in  the venous blood of a human and in the  lungs as all cells produce co2 as a  waste byproduct of metabolism and  changes or fluctuation in co2  concentration can affect the cells  morphology and growth you may for  example see the membranes are not as  tightly attached or you may see granules  in the cytoplasm as an indicator of  toxicity  next relative humidity is provided by  the co2 incubator to prevent evaporation  of water from the growth medium only  water evaporates so that means that the  remaining salts minerals peptides etc  become more concentrated and as that  process continues those can reach toxic  concentrations and we know that  evaporation is four times faster at 80%  humidity then add humidity greater than  93 percent  finally oxygen many if not most people  working with stem cells culture them  under hypoxic conditions because the  oxygen concentration in the stem cell  niche is about one to six percent oxygen  very different than the air we breathe  which is 21% oxygen  we know that when stem cells are grown  in oxygen concentration that mimics the  stem cell niche they grow faster show  less damage to DNA from reactive oxygen  species including for example  chromosomal fusions double strand breaks  and die Center chromosomes most  importantly cells cultured in low oxygen  show normal gene expression  with that understanding of how the  incubator conditions effects themselves  let's look at what happens in the cell  locker system compared to a normal  incubator closed cell lockers do not  experience it when you open a neighbor  neighboring cell locker so here is shown  the environmental conditions in a  typical laboratory room temperature is  20 to 22 degrees Celsius with  atmospheric carbon dioxide concentration  of 0.04 percent and 30 to 60 percent  relative humidity is typical of an  indoor room and then 21% oxygen in the  Earth's atmosphere  now in our newer hair cell Vios  incubator typical incubator settings  would be 37 degrees Celsius 5% co2 93  percent relative humidity or higher and  let's say 1% oxygen but when you open  any incubator door the conditions inside  rush out to mix with the lab air and in  this incubator we see that the incubator  temperature drops to about 30 degrees  co2 drops to below one percent relative  humidity to below 30 percent and oxygen  goes up to 19 to 21 percent all of these