ed by  the co2 incubator to prevent evaporation  of water from the growth medium only  water evaporates so that means that the  remaining salts minerals peptides etc  become more concentrated and as that  process continues those can reach toxic  concentrations and we know that  evaporation is four times faster at 80%  humidity then add humidity greater than  93 percent  finally oxygen many if not most people  working with stem cells culture them  under hypoxic conditions because the  oxygen concentration in the stem cell  niche is about one to six percent oxygen  very different than the air we breathe  which is 21% oxygen  we know that when stem cells are grown  in oxygen concentration that mimics the  stem cell niche they grow faster show  less damage to DNA from reactive oxygen  species including for example  chromosomal fusions double strand breaks  and die Center chromosomes most  importantly cells cultured in low oxygen  show normal gene expression  with that understanding of how the  incubator conditions effects themselves  let's look at what happens in the cell  locker system compared to a normal  incubator closed cell lockers do not  experience it when you open a neighbor  neighboring cell locker so here is shown  the environmental conditions in a  typical laboratory room temperature is  20 to 22 degrees Celsius with  atmospheric carbon dioxide concentration  of 0.04 percent and 30 to 60 percent  relative humidity is typical of an  indoor room and then 21% oxygen in the  Earth's atmosphere  now in our newer hair cell Vios  incubator typical incubator settings  would be 37 degrees Celsius 5% co2 93  percent relative humidity or higher and  let's say 1% oxygen but when you open  any incubator door the conditions inside  rush out to mix with the lab air and in  this incubator we see that the incubator  temperature drops to about 30 degrees  co2 drops to below one percent relative  humidity to below 30 percent and oxygen  goes up to 19 to 21 percent all of these  conditions will be reestablished to the  incubator setting in 10 minutes or so  following a 30 second door opening and  in most incubators recovery is actually  much slower than this for sensitive  cells such as stem cells that door  opening can be very disruptive to their  growth and of course this effect is  multiplied every time you open the  incubator door so if you are sharing an  incubator with others because you don't  have space or funding for multiple  incubators you can see how yourself may  not spend much time at all during the  day at their ideal conditions  now let's look at what happens in the  cell locker system when you open one of  the six cell lockers the closed cell  lockers maintain 37 degrees 5% co2 93  percent humidity and oxygen remains very  low  let me show you in a graphic form that  when you open one cell lock or chamber  the remaining side shaped five chambers  maintain the ideal growth environment  here I'm showing the recovery when  opening one cell locker for thirty  seconds a very long door opening you can  see that the temperature drops and  rapidly recovers to 37 degrees  a similar fast recovery happens for  relative humidity in co2  oxygen in the open cell Locker rises  rapidly from 1% to 21% and then rapidly  drops back down to 1%  now let me add in the conditions in the  cell lockers which were not opened you  can see that it's just a stable flat  line for temperature relative humidity  and co2  the oxygen shows a slight bump of one to  two percent above 1% but this is much  better than rising all the way to 21% we  wanted to show an extreme example of  culturing at 1% oxygen but most of you  are probably culturing it 3 to 5% oxygen  and would not see much of a change at  all for sensitive stem cells this  provides a much more stable environment  than culturing in a standard incubator  and for oxygen we know that oxygen  affects nearly every cellular process so  this is really critical for proper stem  cell growth  another big benefit of the cell locker  system is that it reduces gas use and  evaporation by at least 50 percent  depending on the conditions required  compared to the same incubator design  that does not incorporate the cell  lockers  so first let me show you data for gas  use we looked at nitrogen saving in a  hypoxic incubator because keeping oxygen  at one to six percent instead of  atmospheric 21% uses a lot of injected  nitrogen gas if you never open any of  the doors from the cell locker system as  shown in the first line in this table  then the cell locker system uses  slightly more gas because all of those  gaskets around the individual doors let  a little gas through but in a more  realistic scenario where you are opening  the doors throughout the day you can see  that depending on the number of openings  and the oxygen concentration you would  use at least 50% less gas  so if we look at the most common example  for six thirty second door openings  using three percent oxygen this scenario  the cell locker system uses 60% less  nitrogen so for a typical lab this tank  of nitrogen would last 14 days instead  of five point seven days a significant  savings  now let's look at evaporation we tested  evaporation using 15 grams of water in  three dishes each in all six cell  lockers and found an average evaporation  rate of 0.6 grams of water loss per day  and you can see that it's very uniform  across all of the cell lockers that  evaporation rate compares to our  standard Harris cell vios incubator  which itself has very low evaporation  with a rate of 1.3 grams of water loss  per day so if you are using a lot of  96-well and 384-well screening assays  and you struggle with edge effect or  drying this cell our system savings of  more than 50% reduction in evaporation  is a benefit to you and using 50% less  carbon dioxide or nitrogen gas is a  benefit to everyone  now we did have a few labs do beta  testing on the cell locker system for us  and one of those labs works with induced  pluripotent stem cells they had worked  very hard to optimize their system and  they work in a GMP cleanroom so  everything is carefully documented  making them an ideal beta tester they  tested the cell locker system side by  side with their optimized incubator  producing and growing IPS cells they  found that their IPS cells showed the  same quality when cultured in the cell  locker system compared to the optimized  incubator in both cases they tested and  compared more than 50 colonies finding  zero spots of differentiation in either  case  next they harvested cryopreserved each  cell from each system when they thawed  and regrew the cells in their optimized  system and in the cell locker system  they found that the IPS cell colonies  showed the same quality  taken together this work shows that the  materials and environmental conditions  in the cell locker system can right out  of the box provide the ideal conditions  obtained after a lot of time and  investment in a carefully designed eight  system so much less time an empirical  optimization would be required when  bringing a cell locker system into a lab  in summary I hope I have shown you that  there are great reasons to use the cell  locker system to grow your stem cells  including protection from contamination  preservation of the ideal growth  environment and reduce gas use in  evaporation the cell locker system is  patented and available exclusively in  hair cell bios and for most areas stereo  cycle co2 incubators thank you very much  for your attention and I wish you the  very best success in your work
 

